cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite this repository and the sources listed under
  `references` — in particular the MaxASA table behind any RSA value you report.
title: "foldenv: per-residue structural context from AlphaFold"
abstract: >-
  Computes a protein residue's structural microenvironment from its AlphaFold-predicted
  fold: relative solvent accessibility (RSA), three-state secondary structure via DSSP,
  spatial contacts and packing, a pLDDT confidence flag, and an optional per-residue
  protein-language-model embedding. One call takes a UniProt accession and residue
  position and returns a strict-JSON-safe record, enough to separate a buried structural
  residue from a functional surface residue or to featurize a mutation site.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Chin
    given-names: Connor
    email: cchin29@stanford.edu
    affiliation: Stanford University
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-08-02"
license: CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
license-url: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/"
url: "https://github.com/cchin29/foldenv"
repository-code: "https://github.com/cchin29/foldenv"
# `version` and `date-released` track the git tag and `pyproject.toml` together. A CITATION.cff
# naming a version the package does not is worse than one naming none, and a date ahead of the
# tag claims an artifact nobody can fetch. Bump all three in the release commit.
#
# No `identifiers:` block yet -- there is no DOI for the software itself. If one is minted from
# the v0.1.0 tag, add it here in the same pass.
#
# `issue`, not `number`, for a periodical issue: CFF 1.2 defines `number` as the accession
# number of a work, which is not what a journal issue is. Long author lists are truncated to
# the first three plus the senior author, and every truncated entry says so in its `notes`.
keywords:
  - protein structure
  - AlphaFold
  - relative solvent accessibility
  - RSA
  - secondary structure
  - DSSP
  - residue contacts
  - contact map
  - pLDDT
  - residue microenvironment
  - protein language model
  - per-residue embedding
  - bioinformatics
  - structural bioinformatics
  - mutation effect
references:
  # Attribution required by licence, not by convention: foldenv/constants.py and foldenv/plm.py
  # adapt from MuLAN, which is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. constants.py takes lookup tables and part of the
  # PLM registry (data); plm.py adapts routines. See NOTICE for the exact list and its carve-outs.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      MuLAN: Mutation-driven Light Attention Networks for investigating
      protein-protein interactions from sequences
    authors:
      - family-names: Lombardi
        given-names: Gianluca
      - family-names: Carbone
        given-names: Alessandra
    year: 2024
    doi: 10.1101/2024.08.24.609515
    journal: bioRxiv
    notes: >-
      Upstream work that two helper modules here adapt from, and whose licence this package
      inherits. Code: https://github.com/GianLMB/mulan

  # The primary input. AlphaFold DB asks for the method paper and the database paper separately.
  - type: article
    title: "Highly accurate protein structure prediction with AlphaFold"
    authors:
      - family-names: Jumper
        given-names: John
      - family-names: Evans
        given-names: Richard
      - family-names: Pritzel
        given-names: Alexander
      - family-names: Hassabis
        given-names: Demis
    year: 2021
    doi: 10.1038/s41586-021-03819-2
    journal: Nature
    volume: 596
    start: 583
    end: 589
    notes: "First three authors plus the senior author; see the paper for the full list."
  - type: article
    title: >-
      AlphaFold Protein Structure Database 2025: a redesigned interface and updated
      structural coverage
    authors:
      - family-names: Bertoni
        given-names: Damian
      - family-names: Tsenkov
        given-names: Maxim
      - family-names: Magana
        given-names: Paulyna
      - family-names: Velankar
        given-names: Sameer
    year: 2025
    doi: 10.1093/nar/gkaf1226
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 54
    issue: D1
    start: D358
    end: D362
    notes: >-
      The database foldenv/fetch.py queries at https://alphafold.ebi.ac.uk/api/prediction, and
      the paper AlphaFold DB currently asks users to cite. It supersedes the 2024 database
      paper (10.1093/nar/gkad1011). First three authors plus the senior author.

  # A second structural input: validation.crystal_crosscheck downloads experimental structures
  # from RCSB to check the AlphaFold-derived RSA and secondary structure against a crystal.
  - type: article
    title: "The Protein Data Bank"
    authors:
      - family-names: Berman
        given-names: Helen M.
      - family-names: Westbrook
        given-names: John
      - family-names: Feng
        given-names: Zukang
      - family-names: Bourne
        given-names: Philip E.
    year: 2000
    doi: 10.1093/nar/28.1.235
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 28
    issue: 1
    start: 235
    end: 242
    notes: "First three authors plus the senior author; see the paper for the full list."

  # Secondary structure and the ABSOLUTE solvent accessibility that RSA is computed from.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Dictionary of protein secondary structure: pattern recognition of hydrogen-bonded
      and geometrical features
    authors:
      - family-names: Kabsch
        given-names: Wolfgang
      - family-names: Sander
        given-names: Christian
    year: 1983
    doi: 10.1002/bip.360221211
    journal: Biopolymers
    volume: 22
    issue: 12
    start: 2577
    end: 2637
  - type: article
    title: "A series of PDB-related databanks for everyday needs"
    authors:
      - family-names: Touw
        given-names: Wouter G.
      - family-names: Baakman
        given-names: Coos
      - family-names: Black
        given-names: Jon
      - family-names: te Beek
        given-names: Tim A. H.
      - family-names: Krieger
        given-names: Elmar
      - family-names: Joosten
        given-names: Robbie P.
      - family-names: Vriend
        given-names: Gert
    year: 2015
    doi: 10.1093/nar/gku1028
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 43
    start: D364
    end: D368
    notes: "The DSSP v4 line that provides the `mkdssp` binary this package shells out to."

  # The MaxASA reference tables. RSA = DSSP absolute ASA / MaxASA, so a reported RSA is a claim
  # about a specific table: absolute values shift ~0.05-0.1 between them. `rsa.max_asa_table`
  # selects one and records the choice; both shipped options are cited here.
  - type: article
    title: "Maximum Allowed Solvent Accessibilites of Residues in Proteins"
    authors:
      - family-names: Tien
        given-names: Matthew Z.
      - family-names: Meyer
        given-names: Austin G.
      - family-names: Sydykova
        given-names: Dariya K.
      - family-names: Spielman
        given-names: Stephanie J.
      - family-names: Wilke
        given-names: Claus O.
    year: 2013
    doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0080635
    journal: PLoS ONE
    volume: 8
    issue: 11
    start: e80635
    notes: >-
      Title verbatim from the published article, including the "Accessibilites" spelling --
      do not correct it. Supplies the default `tien2013_theoretical` table and the
      `tien2013_empirical` alternative.
  - type: article
    title: "Conservation and prediction of solvent accessibility in protein families"
    authors:
      - family-names: Rost
        given-names: Burkhard
      - family-names: Sander
        given-names: Chris
    year: 1994
    doi: 10.1002/prot.340200303
    journal: Proteins
    volume: 20
    issue: 3
    start: 216
    end: 226
    notes: >-
      Supplies the `sander_rost1994` MaxASA table. The config key inverts the published author
      order, which is Rost & Sander; the key is kept as-is because renaming it would break
      existing configs.

  # The substitution matrix behind the crystal cross-check alignment. Every number in
  # CrosscheckReport depends on the residue mapping this matrix produces.
  - type: article
    title: "Amino acid substitution matrices from protein blocks"
    authors:
      - family-names: Henikoff
        given-names: Steven
      - family-names: Henikoff
        given-names: Jorja G.
    year: 1992
    doi: 10.1073/pnas.89.22.10915
    journal: Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences
    volume: 89
    issue: 22
    start: 10915
    end: 10919
    notes: "BLOSUM62, loaded in validation.py to align the AlphaFold and crystal sequences."

  # Parses the structures, drives mkdssp, supplies the KD-tree behind contact_count, and runs
  # the crystal-crosscheck alignment.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Biopython: freely available Python tools for computational molecular biology
      and bioinformatics
    authors:
      - family-names: Cock
        given-names: Peter J. A.
      - family-names: Antao
        given-names: Tiago
      - family-names: Chang
        given-names: Jeffrey T.
      - family-names: de Hoon
        given-names: Michiel J. L.
    year: 2009
    doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/btp163
    journal: Bioinformatics
    volume: 25
    issue: 11
    start: 1422
    end: 1423
    notes: >-
      More than plumbing here: `dssp_dict_from_pdb_file` and the mmCIF/PDB parsers, the
      `NeighborSearch` KD-tree behind `contact_count` and `nearest_contacts`, and the
      `PairwiseAligner` behind the crystal cross-check. First three authors plus the senior
      author; see the paper for the full list.

  # The 3Di structural alphabet, computed from the AlphaFold backbone by mini3di. This is an
  # algorithm from a paper producing half of every SaProt token, not a packaging detail --
  # `mini3di` being an optional [saprot] extra does not change what it implements.
  - type: article
    title: "Fast and accurate protein structure search with Foldseek"
    authors:
      - family-names: van Kempen
        given-names: Michel
      - family-names: Kim
        given-names: Stephanie S.
      - family-names: Tumescheit
        given-names: Charlotte
      - family-names: Steinegger
        given-names: Martin
    year: 2024
    doi: 10.1038/s41587-023-01773-0
    journal: Nature Biotechnology
    volume: 42
    start: 243
    end: 246
    notes: >-
      Source of the 3Di alphabet that mini3di implements. First three authors plus the senior
      author; see the paper for the full list.

  # The default embedding backbone. `get_structural_context` computes an embedding unless asked
  # not to, and `embedding`/`embedding_model` are required keys in the output schema -- so Ankh
  # is what a caller who makes no choice gets, which makes it a component rather than an option.
  - type: article
    title: "Ankh: Optimized Protein Language Model Unlocks General-Purpose Modelling"
    authors:
      - family-names: Elnaggar
        given-names: Ahmed
      - family-names: Essam
        given-names: Hazem
      - family-names: Salah-Eldin
        given-names: Wafaa
      - family-names: Rost
        given-names: Burkhard
    year: 2023
    doi: 10.1101/2023.01.16.524265
    journal: bioRxiv
    notes: >-
      Ankh-large, the default `embedding_model`. The published title carries an ankh glyph
      after "Ankh", omitted here for tooling safety. First three authors plus the senior author.

# The nine other selectable backbones -- Ankh3-large/xl, ProstT5, SaProt 650M/1.3B, ESM2-3B/650M,
# ESM C 600M/6B -- are not cited here. Only the default is a component; the rest are chosen by
# the caller, they collapse to a handful of model families, and listing them would bury the
# sources above. `EMBEDDING_MODELS` in foldenv/embedding.py names each one, and
# `PLM_ENCODERS` in foldenv/constants.py maps it to its HuggingFace Hub id, which is where the
# upstream model and its paper are found. If a published result depends on one of them, cite
# that model as well as this package.
